  "raw": " assist in the determination of the number of animals that are sick. For example, a particular indicator may be indicated in the form of a specific color or a specific type of enzyme or other specific material. Thus to be truly useful for veterinary medicine, such animal indicators must have more than one or two characteristics that make them useful, at a minimum, for veterinary diagnostics.\nThe most commonly used indicators for disease diagnostic tests are colored reagents. Examples of colored reagents include pigments such as dye, oil, oil-soluble metal salts, chelates, thiols, acids, amines or alcohols, and the like. Color may also be generated by the interaction of these reagents with organic substrates. Such organic substrates include, but are not limited to, fatty acids, fatty alcohols, ester, and polymeric substrates. The reagents themselves are typically obtained from sources such as microorganisms and animal tissues.\nThe indicators that have been developed are",
